WEBVTT

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.500
Most people give their assistant a notepad tool.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.770
The model stops mid-turn, emits a tool call,

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:08.000
waits for the result, then keeps going.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.000
It works.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.680
But every note costs a full,

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.000
extra round trip through the model.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Fine on a demo.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.000
At scale, you feel it.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.000
We do it differently.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
We don't give the assistant a tool.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.000
We give it a convention.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.000
It writes its notes inline.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.000
Inside the response, it's already generating.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Wrapped in start and end sentinels.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.000
A scratchpad block.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.550
Fenced by delimiters, your parser can't confuse with

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.000
prose. That's the whole trick.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.000
The notes ride the response stream.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
No tool call, no round trip,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.000
no second inference hop.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.480
The model is generating that text token by token

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.830
anyway. So the notes come straight off the stream

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.000
into the chat history.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
A tool call is a synchronous detour.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
The model stops, the runtime executes,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.000
the model resumes.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.200
And that loop costs latency,

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.000
and more subtly, coherence.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Inline notes are asynchronous and free.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.000
The model never breaks stride.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.070
To recognize the block, the runtime just runs a

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:13.000
streaming parse over the token delta.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.140
A small state machine that flips to recording on

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.000
the open sentinel and done on the close.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Here's the part that trips people up.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
At capture time, we don't persist anything.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.650
The notes are already in the chat history,

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:31.000
already in context, exactly where the reasoning happens.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.000
That's working memory.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Nothing gets written yet.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Persistence happens later, at eviction.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Tip 8.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Three details that matter.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.000
One, hide the block from the UI,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.000
but keep it on the record.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Hidden output is not deleted state.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.480
Two, there's a small per turn token tax for

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.000
the sentinels and the block.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.420
Budget for it, it's the cheapest durable state you'll

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.000
ever get from a model.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.600
Three, you can push the buffer to the infrastructure

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.000
layer so the model can't forget to do it.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.310
But just emitting the tags works and is far

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.000
less to maintain.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:11.840
Start there.
